WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:04.770
Stephen King's The Dark Tower has finally
arrived in theaters after a long and complicated

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:06.089
journey to the big screen.

00:00:06.089 --> 00:00:10.760
However, Hollywood's latest attempt to launch
a cinematic universe didn't exactly go as

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:15.540
planned: the movie was widely panned by critics,
and opened to a disappointing $19.5 million

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:16.540
haul.

00:00:16.540 --> 00:00:19.660
This was still good enough for first place,
but the movie's lackluster performance with

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:24.060
critics and audiences can only be seen as
a disappointment — for both the studio as

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:27.849
well as for King fans who'd waited years to
see the books brought to life.

00:00:27.849 --> 00:00:30.640
Here's what went wrong with The Dark Tower's
big screen debut.

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:35.760
And a friendly warning: spoilers for both
the movies — and the books — lie ahead.

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:37.930
Source material misread

00:00:37.930 --> 00:00:41.470
The many, many stories King penned in the
Dark Tower universe spanned quite a lot of

00:00:41.470 --> 00:00:46.070
plot, but the studio decided to mix and match
elements from all of them, which still wound

00:00:46.070 --> 00:00:48.859
up being a sequel to the books, rather than
an adaptation.

00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:55.840
"We actually took the world and sort've stitched
it a little bit together, without losing the

00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:57.480
promise of greater adventures."

00:00:58.010 --> 00:01:01.600
The groundwork was laid at the end of the
seventh book of King's saga, when our hero

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:06.000
Roland Deschain finally reaches the top of
the Dark Tower and realizes he'd been on this

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:08.439
long journey many times before.

00:01:08.439 --> 00:01:11.320
While picking the movie up where the final
book leaves off may have seemed like a smart

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:16.049
way for the filmmakers to boil down the novels'
epic scale into just one film, it was also

00:01:16.049 --> 00:01:19.951
alienating to the hardcore fans who'd been
hoping to see the actual events of the novels

00:01:19.951 --> 00:01:22.350
themselves play out onscreen.

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:25.950
The Dark Tower also straddled the difficult
line of trying to appeal to those who hadn't

00:01:25.950 --> 00:01:29.070
read any of the books and those who'd read
every word.

00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:33.390
That effort to please fans and make the content
digestible enough for new audiences simply

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:37.700
didn't work, adding up to a movie that didn't
give anyone what they were looking for.

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:41.700
While the movie did a fair job of setting
up its central quest story, it failed to sell

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:46.979
the deeper conflicts inherent in the epic
journey of the characters and felt incomplete.

00:01:46.979 --> 00:01:48.640
Speaking of which …

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:49.640
Run time rut

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:53.970
Nowadays, viewers who walk into big-budget
blockbusters expect to be in the theaters

00:01:53.970 --> 00:01:56.460
for at least two hours — and often more.

00:01:56.460 --> 00:01:59.840
The Dark Tower easily could have filled up
this runtime — in fact, it probably would

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:03.740
have helped its awkward attempt to fit so
many disparate elements from King's novels

00:02:03.740 --> 00:02:04.810
into one movie.

00:02:04.810 --> 00:02:07.820
"They have guns and bullets in your world?"

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:10.500
"You're gonna like earth a lot."

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:15.420
Director Nick Arcel, however, decided to instead
keep the film at a very tight 95 minutes.

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:19.120
The director said he did this because he saw
the film as an "introduction" to the world

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:22.590
of the novels, adding that the movie had a
"lean" script that would introduce the basic

00:02:22.590 --> 00:02:26.090
ideas of the project without being too overwhelming
at first.

00:02:26.090 --> 00:02:30.430
While this runtime may have been doable had
Arcel chosen to only focus on the first book,

00:02:30.430 --> 00:02:34.360
the movie's decision to mix-and-match elements
from all of King's books as a pseudo-sequel

00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:38.280
meant it had to introduce huge amounts of
information while playing out its story.

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:42.440
Although Arcel saw the short runtime as a
way to build the world without doing too much,

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:46.989
some saw it as being too brief, leaving the
world feeling empty and unfinished.

00:02:46.989 --> 00:02:52.300
The movie could have benefitted from an extra
30 minutes of world-building — if not more.

00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:53.300
Filmmaking fracas

00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:57.580
Arcel wasn't the first director to try his
hand at adapting The Dark Tower.

00:02:57.580 --> 00:03:02.700
In 2007, J.J. Abrams pulled together his Lost
pals Carlton Cuse and Damon Lindelof, who

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:04.700
were also big fans of Stephen King.

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:08.069
But they ended up letting go of the adaptation
option because they were too nervous about

00:03:08.069 --> 00:03:09.710
messing up the beloved work.

00:03:09.710 --> 00:03:13.599
That's when Ron Howard stepped up to the plate
with a plan for three movies and a TV series

00:03:13.599 --> 00:03:15.010
to to bring the story to life.

00:03:15.010 --> 00:03:18.840
The project was set up at Universal Pictures,
but the studio pulled the plug on that expensive

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:22.680
idea, and it took three more years for the
movie rights to find another home with Media

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:24.990
Rights Capital and Sony Pictures.

00:03:24.990 --> 00:03:27.560
But that wasn't the end of the behind-the-scenes
drama.

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:32.110
MRC and Sony were reportedly unhappy with
an early cut of the film, and considered bringing

00:03:32.110 --> 00:03:34.750
in a more experienced director to do another
cut.

00:03:34.750 --> 00:03:38.239
That cost would've been too high, though,
so they shelled out funding for re-shoots

00:03:38.239 --> 00:03:40.270
amid creative collisions.

00:03:40.270 --> 00:03:44.670
All that infighting led to the release date
being pushed back a whopping three times,

00:03:44.670 --> 00:03:47.660
which probably tipped fans off to how troubled
this production was.

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:49.870
"The tower will fall."

00:03:49.870 --> 00:03:51.010
Bad buzz

00:03:51.010 --> 00:03:55.210
Whether it was a result of behind-the-scenes
tumult or just a strategy, advertising for

00:03:55.210 --> 00:03:57.560
The Dark Tower ended up being rather minimal.

00:03:57.560 --> 00:04:00.970
The first trailer didn't arrive until early
May, three months before the film's release

00:04:00.970 --> 00:04:04.760
and was overshadowed by other, more high-profile
genre movies.

00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:08.310
By being stingy with promotional materials,
the studios may have been trying to avoid

00:04:08.310 --> 00:04:10.950
over-saturation, but that plan clearly backfired.

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:15.000
Two weeks before its release, it failed to
even track in the top 10 movies being talked

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:19.200
about on social media -- despite a largely
ignored video game offering — and the only

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:24.420
thing that really got people talking was when
critics completely eviscerated the movie.

00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:28.500
It's debatable how much influence bad reviews
actually have on audiences, but the fact that

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:32.570
this film is dangerously close to a one-digit
Rotten Tomatoes rating probably didn't encourage

00:04:32.570 --> 00:04:36.030
people who were on the fence about the seeing
the pic to buy a ticket.

00:04:36.030 --> 00:04:37.200
Turning to TV

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:41.600
The Dark Tower may have been a box office
disappointment, but Sony and MRC still seem

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:44.120
to be moving ahead with at least one aspect
of the franchise.

00:04:44.120 --> 00:04:47.730
While there's no word yet on whether the planned
movie sequels will move forward, the TV show

00:04:47.730 --> 00:04:51.220
made a major step just before the film was
released by adding The Walking Dead's Glen

00:04:51.220 --> 00:04:52.860
Mazzara as a showrunner.

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:56.920
The TV series is said to be a prequel to the
books, focusing on King's Wizard in Glass

00:04:56.920 --> 00:04:57.920
novel.

00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:00.610
While that means it'll likely have a different
feel than the films, it'll see the return

00:05:00.610 --> 00:05:04.020
of Idris Elba's Roland, Tom Taylor's Jake,
and others.

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:07.580
It's still very early in that process, but
the hiring of Mazzara hints that it could

00:05:07.580 --> 00:05:09.100
be getting off the ground soon.

00:05:09.100 --> 00:05:13.200
The group is hoping to begin production in
2018, so it looks like The Dark Tower hasn't

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:14.200
fallen just yet.

00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:15.580
"You are in very bad shape.

00:05:15.580 --> 00:05:17.250
I'm surprised you're even sitting up."

00:05:17.250 --> 00:05:18.260
"I'm stronger than most."

00:05:18.260 --> 00:05:19.390
Thanks for watching!

00:05:19.390 --> 00:05:22.040
